# What promoting `RETURN`, `EXIT`, `PUSH` and `QUEUE` has to keep.

# EXIT with a value, from a called label, under trace r
# The keyword tag is what makes this `EXIT` rather than `RETURN`: a `Flow`
# that unwound to the activation boundary instead of out of the program would
# resume the caller, print `not reached` and exit 0. Measured to redden when
# `compile` tags an `EXIT`'s op `ReturnKeyword::Return`, on stdout between the
# engines -- **not a unique catch**: the op-stream pins for this op read the
# tag out of the compiled stream, and `run::tests::exit_inside_a_routine_
# ends_the_routine_where_a_labels_exit_ends_the_program` and
# `eval::tests::an_exit_inside_a_routine_reached_by_expression_call_ends_the_
# whole_program` see the wrong flow in a program's own answer.
# It reddens for the `>>>` line too, when `Interp::returned_value` stops
# emitting it -- a mutation both engines take, so what sees it here is the
# oracle-measured block rather than the engine comparison. That one is not
# unique either: `tests/trace_oracle/exit_value.rex` reddens with it, and so
# does `run::tests::a_returned_value_traces_in_the_callee_and_again_in_the_
# caller`.
program
trace r
call zsub
say 'not reached'
exit
zsub:
exit 5
----
rc> 5
err>      2 *-* call zsub
err>      5 *-*   zsub:
err>      6 *-*   exit 5
err>        >>>     "5"

# PUSH and QUEUE interleaved, read back by PARSE PULL, under trace r
# The read-back is what makes the two spellings tell each other apart: three
# lines written `c`, `b`, `a` come back `a`, `c`, `b`, which is the LIFO/FIFO
# interleaving and not any one end used twice.
# The bare `QUEUE` is the second half: it queues a null string **and** traces
# it, so the `>>>   ""` and the `[]` from the line that reads it back are one
# clause's evidence that the bare form is not a skipped clause.
# Measured to redden when `compile` tags a `QUEUE`'s op `QueueKeyword::Push`
# -- `a b c` where the oracle answers `a c b` -- which is not a unique catch:
# the op-stream pin, the extracted-program sweep, `pull_queue_covers_the_line_
# reading_sources_in_both_modes` and the exempt-set gate all redden with it.
# **The `>>>` line is a unique catch**, and that is measured rather than
# argued: with `Interp::queue_evaluated` emitting no trace line, this row is
# the only red test in the workspace, and with this file held out of the
# directory the whole workspace is green under the same mutation. Both engines
# take that mutation, so the engine comparison cannot see it and the
# oracle-measured block is what does; `corpus/lang/push_queue.rex` traces the
# same lines but is compared against the oracle only in a report-mode harness.
program
trace r
push 'c'
queue 'b'
push 'a'
parse pull za
parse pull zb
parse pull zc
say za zb zc
queue
parse pull zd
say '[' || zd || ']'
exit
----
rc> 0
out> a c b
out> []
err>      2 *-* push 'c'
err>        >>>   "c"
err>      3 *-* queue 'b'
err>        >>>   "b"
err>      4 *-* push 'a'
err>        >>>   "a"
err>      5 *-* parse pull za
err>        >K>   "PULL" => "a"
err>        >>>   "a"
err>        >>>   "a"
err>      6 *-* parse pull zb
err>        >K>   "PULL" => "c"
err>        >>>   "c"
err>        >>>   "c"
err>      7 *-* parse pull zc
err>        >K>   "PULL" => "b"
err>        >>>   "b"
err>        >>>   "b"
err>      8 *-* say za zb zc
err>        >>>   "a c b"
err>      9 *-* queue
err>        >>>   ""
err>     10 *-* parse pull zd
err>        >K>   "PULL" => ""
err>        >>>   ""
err>        >>>   ""
err>     11 *-* say '[' || zd || ']'
err>        >>>   "[]"
err>     12 *-* exit

# RETURN '' against a bare RETURN, and what the caller's RESULT reads
# The row `src: Option<u16>` exists for. `RETURN ''` sets `RESULT` to the null
# string and traces it; a bare `RETURN` leaves `RESULT` unset, which an
# unset Rexx variable renders as its own uppercased name -- so `after bare:
# RESULT` is the whole discrimination, and a compiled form that handed the
# bare clause a register holding an empty value would print `after bare: `
# for both.
# Measured with this row as the only stanza in the directory: it reddens when
# the compiled `Op::Return` drops the value it was handed, printing `after
# empty: RESULT` where the oracle prints `after empty: `. Not a unique catch
# -- that mutation reddens the extracted-program sweep and the branch-shape
# table as well. The degenerate implementation the paragraph above describes
# was measured too, by giving the bare form a register holding the null
# string: what catches that first is the arity `debug_assert` in
# `Op::Return`'s own driver arm, not any row here.
program
trace r
call zempty
say 'after empty:' result
call zbare
say 'after bare:' result
exit
zempty:
return ''
zbare:
return
----
rc> 0
out> after empty: 
out> after bare: RESULT
err>      2 *-* call zempty
err>      7 *-*   zempty:
err>      8 *-*   return ''
err>        >>>     ""
err>        >>>   ""
err>      3 *-* say 'after empty:' result
err>        >>>   "after empty: "
err>      4 *-* call zbare
err>      9 *-*   zbare:
err>     10 *-*   return
err>      5 *-* say 'after bare:' result
err>        >>>   "after bare: RESULT"
err>      6 *-* exit

# a call inside a RETURN's expression, under trace i
# The call takes an `Op::CallExpr` addressed at slot `0`, which is the descent
# `Interp::chunk_node_at`'s own arm for these instructions resolves; without
# that arm the driver reaches `Loud::call_op_off_its_node` instead of calling
# anything.
# Measured to redden when that arm loses its `RETURN`/`EXIT`/`PUSH`/`QUEUE`
# alternatives -- `got 5` against nothing at all -- and **not a unique
# catch**: the extracted-program sweep, the clause-value boundary witness and
# the exempt-set gate redden under it too.
# The intermediate lines and their order are the rest of what is pinned: the
# argument, the function's own `>F>`, the right operand, the operator, the
# `RETURN`'s own `>>>`, and the caller's second `>>>` for the same value at
# its own indent.
program
trace i
call zsub
say 'got' result
exit
zsub:
return length('abcd') + 1
----
rc> 0
out> got 5
err>      2 *-* call zsub
err>      5 *-*   zsub:
err>      6 *-*   return length('abcd') + 1
err>        >L>     "abcd"
err>        >A>     "abcd"
err>        >F>     LENGTH => "4"
err>        >L>     "1"
err>        >O>     "+" => "5"
err>        >>>     "5"
err>        >>>   "5"
err>      3 *-* say 'got' result
err>        >L>   "got"
err>        >V>   RESULT => "5"
err>        >O>   " " => "got 5"
err>        >>>   "got 5"
err>      4 *-* exit
